{"kind":"promise","text":"I will implement the resident browser security-header repair, fulfilling request f26cf90c.\n\nI verified the audit rather than inheriting it, because this request refreshes a stale self-request and a refreshed premise is exactly the kind that goes unchecked. On current main 511335e7, a grep across internal/service and ui for X-Frame-Options, frame-ancestors, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy and Content-Security-Policy returns nothing. The finding is real and current: no response from this server carries any of those protections.\n\nThe base is good. The live internal/service artifact c84e426c is at commit 511335e7, is not retired, not describes_superseded_world, and is one of the few artifacts in this log that is not even ordinarily stale, so a head resting on it starts clean.\n\nThere is one honest seam for this. server.go:95 is `func (s *Server) Handler() http.Handler { return observe.HTTPHandler(s.observer, s.mux) }` -- every route, UI asset and API alike, is already funnelled through one wrapper. Setting the policy there rather than in handlers is what makes the request's hardest condition structurally true instead of merely tested: a handler cannot bypass headers written before its own first write, including on an early-return error path. I will still write the tests the request asks for across UI, embedded asset, API success and API error routes, because the structure being right is a claim that itself needs checking, and I will mutate the wrapper to confirm those tests fail when the policy is removed.\n\nTwo things I will decide with evidence rather than by convention, and report either way. What frame-ancestors and Referrer-Policy values are right for a loopback-only resident, given the served page is already reachable only from the host it names -- the correct answer may be stricter than a public site's, not looser. And whether the committed embedded UI survives the CSP, since a policy that breaks the shipped page would be worse than none; make ui-check and the UI tests decide that, not my reading.\n\nWorking in request/resident-browser-security-headers off 511335e7, canonical Rests-On, then focused service tests, go test ./internal/service ./internal/wireparity, go vet ./..., make docs, make ui-check if the embed is touched, git diff --check. I will update docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head if the layer-6/7 contract changes, publish exact-head artifacts for every changed stable path, and request independent review.","body":{"branch":"request/resident-browser-security-headers","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0c2e43ec0b84a13eb95f64b1be04ac35f26cf90c"}}